5.3 Solution Components

The framework consists of several independent yet interconnected components.

5.3.1 Source Layer

The Source Layer contains the operational data received from upstream source systems. These datasets represent the latest available business records and serve as the input for daily processing.

Responsibilities:

Receive daily source data.
Provide input for downstream processing.
Maintain latest source records.
5.3.2 Raw Layer

The Raw Layer stores the current day's source data exactly as received from upstream systems.

Responsibilities:

Preserve incoming source data.
Act as the primary input for comparison.
Provide current business records.
5.3.3 Previous Raw Layer

The Previous Raw Layer stores the previous execution's source data.

This layer enables efficient comparison between the current and previous datasets.

Responsibilities:

Store previous day's source data.
Enable incremental change detection.
Support Insert, Update, and Delete identification.
5.3.4 Staging Layer

The Staging Layer performs row-level comparison between Raw and Previous Raw data.

Based on the comparison, each record is classified as:

Insert
Update
Delete

The processed records are written into staging tables, which become the source for downstream History and Snapshot processing.

Responsibilities:

Compare Raw vs Previous Raw.
Detect data changes.
Populate staging tables.
Generate staging logs.
5.3.5 History Layer

The History Layer maintains complete historical versions of business records using the Slowly Changing Dimension Type 2 (SCD Type 2) methodology.

Instead of overwriting existing records, previous versions are retained while new versions are inserted.

Responsibilities:

Preserve historical records.
Maintain Active and Inactive versions.
Support historical reporting.
Enable data auditing.
5.3.6 Snapshot Layer

The Snapshot Layer maintains the latest version of each business record.

Unlike History, Snapshot stores only the current state of the data.

Responsibilities:

Maintain current business data.
Replace outdated records.
Provide reporting datasets.
Support operational dashboards.
5.3.7 Logging Framework

The Logging Framework records execution information for every processed table.

Information captured includes:

Processing status
Execution timestamps
Record counts
Retry attempts
Error details
Processing duration

The logs provide complete operational visibility into pipeline execution.

5.3.8 Audit Framework

The Audit Framework provides centralized monitoring and validation of all pipeline executions.

It enables:

Daily monitoring
Failure analysis
Operational reporting
Data validation
Processing statistics
