5.6 Metadata-Driven Design

Instead of writing separate code for every source table, the framework uses metadata to drive execution.

The metadata contains information such as:

Table Name
Primary Key Column(s)
Source Table
Target Table
Processing Configuration

Using this approach:

The same processing logic is reused across all tables.
New tables can be onboarded with minimal effort.
Maintenance becomes significantly easier.
5.7 Parallel Processing

To improve performance, tables are processed concurrently using a thread pool.

Each table is executed independently.

Benefits include:

Reduced execution time.
Better utilization of Spark resources.
Failure isolation.
Improved scalability.
5.8 Error Handling Strategy

The framework includes a robust error handling mechanism.

If processing fails:

The exception is captured.
Retry logic is initiated.
Processing is attempted again.
Failure information is written into log tables.
Remaining tables continue processing.

This prevents a single table failure from stopping the complete pipeline.

5.9 Timezone Strategy

The framework standardizes timestamp handling to ensure consistency across executions.

Business processing follows the configured CST/CDT timezone.
Log tables additionally capture UTC timestamps to support global operational monitoring.
This dual-timezone approach improves traceability across different geographic regions while preserving business reporting requirements.
5.10 Architecture Highlights
Feature	Description
Processing Engine	PySpark on Microsoft Fabric
Storage	Delta Lakehouse
Design Pattern	Metadata Driven
Change Detection	Row-Level Comparison
History	SCD Type 2
Snapshot	Latest Current State
Processing Mode	Incremental
Parallel Execution	ThreadPoolExecutor
Logging	Daily and Final Logs
Retry Logic	Automatic Retry Mechanism
Monitoring	Audit Framework
Scalability	Supports 500+ Tables
Backfill	Automatic Pending Date Processing
Timezone	CST/CDT for business processing and UTC for operational logging
